rence/closeout-20261006.md`, commit as docs. The morning\nreader is the operator — write for them: what they must do tomorrow is PROVISION\nSIGNALWIRE, send themselves a code, and run the real redemption; say which of tonight's\ngates proved what, and which attended act each KR still waits on.\n\nYour FINAL CHAT MESSAGE is a hand-off, so CLAUDE.md §\"Context checkpoints at\nhand-offs\" binds it. Do not end on a results summary. End with, in this order:\n1. Next steps, led by morning triage — what is waiting, what is parked, what the\n   operator has to decide. Run `night-crew next` after the closeout is written and\n   report its answer beneath the hand-off: its triage precondition reads a parked\n   fork OR a closeout with no reference/triage-<runid>.md beside it (§15cl.272), so\n   the closeout you just wrote is what makes this night visible to it, and the two\n   normally agree. The one case where they do not is a night that never closed —\n   then neither half has anything to read; name the morning triage yourself and say\n   why the order cannot.\n2. The context-checkpoint verdict with its reason. For a finished run it is\n   🔄 Clear now (recommended) — the morning ritual is designed to start cold and\n   reviews the run branch on its merits; nothing in your context is load-bearing\n   because HANDOFF.md, DECISIONS-NEEDED.md, and the conflict log carry it all. Say\n   ⛔ instead only if something real is still in flight in this session.\n3. The paste-ready next command: /nc-morning-triage\nWriting HANDOFF.md does NOT discharge this. The file and the closing message are\ndifferent surfaces read at different moments, and the operator reading the last\nthing on screen at 7am is the one this is for.\n.night-crew/knowledge/:\nBACKLOG.md\nCOMMANDS.md\nCONTEXT.md\nbugs.md\ndecisions\ndesign-findings-nightcrew.md\ndesigns\nledger.md\nokrs.md\nprds\npreferences\npreferences.md\nreference\nroadmap.md\nrulebook.md\nscorecard\nspikes\n\n.night-crew/knowledge/reference:\n119