{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfk4jixvZfS7QxyuEC3uH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DEST7aEyFfmdqBgUyab6sT","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"L=.night-crew/runs/2026-10-06-autonomous/logs/identity-code-and-qr; python3 - <<'EOF'\nL='.night-crew/runs/2026-10-06-autonomous/logs/identity-code-and-qr/'\np=L+'mutation-medium-ic01.log'\ns=open(p).read()\na=s.index('# READING:')\ns=s[:a]+'''# READING: red, and NOT at the scanner. At Medium the full payload would be QR version 11, so\n# buildIdentityQR's density bound (never above version 10) shortened the embedded label to fit.\n# On THIS run the test's own independent decode of that image then failed (\"No MultiFormat Readers\n# were able to detect the code\"); on an earlier, unsaved run of the same mutation the image decoded\n# and the test went red on the label instead (\"Free side of w…\" where \"Free side of wings\" was\n# expected). Two different reds for one mutation is itself the finding density-probe.log sizes:\n# whether the vendored decoder reads a given code depends on the individual symbol.\n'''\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\np=L+'density-probe.log'\ns=open(p).read()\ns+='''\n# ── third run, N=150, decoder restricted to QR codes (PROBE_QR_ONLY=1:\n#    new Html5Qrcode(id, {formatsToSupport:[QR_CODE]})) — transcribed from the terminal, not piped to this file ──\nPROBE card: full descriptor, Low, 512px                    len=214  v9/53mod     read 132/150 (88%)  not-read 18  WRONG-TEXT 0\nPROBE control: full descriptor, MEDIUM, 512px              len=214  v11/61mod    read 130/150 (87%)  not-read 20  WRONG-TEXT 0\nPROBE full descriptor, Low, 1024px                         len=214  v9/53mod     read 131/150 (87%)  not-read 19  WRONG-TEXT 0\nPROBE full descriptor, Low, 488px (8px modules exactly)    len=214  v9/53mod     read 133/150 (89%)  not-read 17  WRONG-TEXT 0\nPROBE descriptor WITHOUT campaign_id, Low, 512px           len=143  v7/45mod     read 146/150 (97%)  not-read 4  WRONG-TEXT 0\nPROBE descriptor label only, Low, 512px                    len=88   v5/37mod     read 143/150 (95%)  not-read 7  WRONG-TEXT 0\nPROBE identity-only (no descriptor), Low, 512px            len=45   v3/29mod     read 141/150 (94%)  not-read 9  WRONG-TEXT 0\n#   1 passed (4.5m)\n\n# ── earlier sweeps of the SAME 30 codes across surface widths (unsaved runs, transcribed) ──\n#   Low / version 9:    px=320 29/30 · 512 29/30 · 640 29/30 · 768 29/30 · 1024 29/30 · 1280 29/30 — the SAME code failed at every width\n#   Medium / version 11: px=320 25/30 · 640 25/30 — the same five codes failed at both widths\n#   through the shipped page's own \"Scan from photo\" input, fresh page load per code: Low 6/8, Medium 7/8\n#   through the shipped page with \"Scan next\" tapped between customers: Low 9/10\n\n# ── READING ──\n# 1. Whether the shipped photo-scan decoder reads an identity code depends on the individual\n#    symbol, and so on the random token. At the card's signed encoding (Low, version 9) it read\n#    89/100 and 123/150 freshly drawn codes: 212 of 250, 85% — about one code in seven is not read\n#    from a pristine PNG, and the same code fails every time.\n# 2. Medium correction (version 11) — the signed spike's \"does not decode\" — read 85/100 and\n#    123/150: 208 of 250, 83%. The two are indistinguishable. The spike drew ONE symbol per variant.\n# 3. The scan surface's width (320 vs 640 vs anything) changed nothing: the decoder already works\n#    on a canvas at least as large as the image.\n# 4. The floor is not this card's payload: a bare identity-only code (45 characters, version 3)\n#    read 93/100, 143/150, 141/150 — about one in seventeen is not read.\n# 5. A decode never produced another customer's token. The \"WRONG-TEXT\" cases are empty strings\n#    from a non-QR format the decoder thought it saw; the scanner shows those as invalidPayload\n#    (nothing rendered), and they vanish when the decoder is restricted to QR.\n# 6. NOT MEASURED: the live-camera path (many frames per second, real optics) — no gate exercises\n#    it — and any decoder other than the vendored one (this box has none).\n'''\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\n\np='tests/marketing-identity.spec.js'\ns=open(p).read()\nold='''// RED-FIRST: written and run against the pre-change tree'''\nnew='''// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n// 🛑 READ BEFORE TRUSTING A RESULT FROM THIS FILE (measured 2026-10-05, run\n// 20261006 — logs/identity-code-and-qr/density-probe.log)\n//\n// These three tests are the signed slate's gates, written as signed. Two of\n// their premises did not survive measurement over 250 freshly drawn codes:\n//\n//   * [IC-03] IS NOT A STABLE PROPERTY. The shipped photo-scan decoder read a\n//     Medium / version-11 code 83% of the time (208 of 250) — as often as it\n//     read the card's own Low / version-9 code (212 of 250). This test passes\n//     only when the one token it happened to mint draws a symbol the decoder\n//     cannot find. Expect it RED on most runs.\n//   * [IC-01] AND [IC-02] PASS ONLY WHEN THE MINTED CODE IS A READABLE ONE —\n//     about six runs in seven. A red here with \"decodeError\" (or the test's own\n//     decode throwing \"No MultiFormat Readers were able to detect the code\") is\n//     that, not a regression. 🛑 playwright.config.js retries once, and a retry\n//     mints a NEW token: a pass on retry is the lottery re-drawn, not a flake\n//     going away.\n//\n// The card is PARKED on this (the encoding was a signed correction; what\n// replaces it is the operator's). Do not \"stabilise\" these tests by pinning a\n// token or adding retries.\n// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n//\n// RED-FIRST: written and run against the pre-change tree'''\nassert s.count(old)==1\nopen(p,'w').write(s.replace(old,new))\nEOF\ngit status --short | grep zz-e1; echo ok","description":"Corre